description:
Build the core of a small command console: methods marked with an attribute become commands, found by reflection, with their arguments parsed from text and help generated automatically.

```csharp
public class Tools
{
    [Command("add", "Adds two whole numbers")]
    public int Add(int a, int b) => a + b;

    [Command("greet", "Says hello")]
    public string Greet(string name, bool shout = false) => shout ? $"HELLO {name.ToUpper()}!" : $"hello {name}";
}

var console = new CommandConsole(new Tools());
console.Run("add 2 40");             // "42"
console.Run("greet Ada");            // "hello Ada"
console.Run("greet Ada true");       // "HELLO ADA!"
console.Run("help");
// add a b: Adds two whole numbers
// greet name [shout]: Says hello
```

Write:

- `[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Method)] public sealed class CommandAttribute(string name, string description) : Attribute` with `Name` and `Description` properties.
- `public class CommandConsole` taking any object in its constructor. It finds the object's public instance methods that have `[Command]`. Two commands with the same name throw `InvalidOperationException` from the constructor.
- `string Run(string line)`:
  - The line is split on whitespace; the first word is the command name. A blank line returns `""`.
  - `help` lists every command sorted by name as `"<name> <params>: <description>"`, joined with `"\n"`. Parameters with default values are shown in square brackets.
  - The remaining words are converted to the parameter types (`int`, `double`, `bool`, `string`) with `Convert.ChangeType(word, type, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)`. Missing words use the parameter's default value.
  - The result is the method's return value as text (`""` for a `void` method).
  - Errors are returned as text, never thrown: `"unknown command: <name>"`, `"usage: <name> <params>"` for too few or too many words, and `"bad value for <param>: <word>"` when a conversion fails.

